Value [Variable]
Select or type in the value, which the Variable shows.
Remarks
The Data type you select determines what you can do.
• You can select the value from a drop-down list.
• You can type in the value of the Variable.
Note
For the data types length, weight, and speed the units you select in the Plant Simulation main
window under File > Model Settings/Preferences > Units > Mass/Speed/Length determine how
Plant Simulation interprets the value.
A value of 100 with the length set to km is interpreted as 100 km.
• Click Open to open the dialog box of the objects that correspond to the data types table, list, stack or
queue.
If the Variable points to a list which is referenced from additional places/objects, the dialog shows the
button Unshare.
Unshare a Variable
To unshare a Variable of data types table, list, stack or queue:
• Click Unshare to make Plant Simulation assign its own copy of the list to the Variable instead of the
shared contents of the list. This corresponds to the method unshare.
Value [Variable]
11-3768 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Click 
  and select the object of data type object in the dialog Select Object.
• Or type the path to and the name of the object into the text box Value.
Note
F2 opens the dialog of the object, whose name you typed into the text box.
The data type object designates an absolute or a relative path to an object.
◦ The absolute path always starts with a period, starting in the Class Library, such as .building and
identifies the object uniquely. Plant Simulation does not change the absolute path if you derive or
insert a Frame using a Variable object, i.e., the absolute path will still reference the source object, not
to the one you derived or inserted!
◦ The relative path which starts with Location or ~, considers the current location of the Variable
object each time the object Variable is accessed. Thus, when deriving or inserting an object consisting
of several hierarchically structured Frames, you do not have to change the existing object values in
the Variable objects.
• To use the data type time with a random number distribution, select the data type randtime, and type
in the parameters of the distribution function, as the data type Time only accepts constant values. Note
you have to initialize the randtime value before accessing it, for example by employing the method
rollDice, which determines the next random number that the Variable uses.
